Q1. What is Test-Driven Development (TDD)?

Interview Ready Answer

Test-Driven Development (TDD) is a software development methodology
in which developers write automated test cases before writing the
actual production code.

Development follows the Red → Green → Refactor cycle to produce
reliable, maintainable, and well-tested software.

Key Points

• Test First approach.
• Tests are written before implementation.
• Produces reliable code.
• Encourages continuous testing.
• Improves maintainability.

======================================================================

Q2. Why do we need TDD?

Interview Ready Answer

TDD helps detect defects early by writing tests before code.
It reduces debugging effort, improves software quality, and gives
developers confidence that new changes do not break existing features.

Advantages

• Finds bugs early.
• Improves code quality.
• Easier debugging.
• Safer refactoring.
• Better maintainability.
• Higher developer confidence.

Real-Life Example

ATM Withdrawal

Requirement:
Withdraw ₹1000

↓

Write Test

↓

Expected Balance = ₹4000

↓

Test Fails

↓

Write Minimum Code

↓

Test Passes

Instead of debugging after writing the complete feature.

======================================================================

Q3. Explain the Red → Green → Refactor Cycle.

Interview Ready Answer

The Red → Green → Refactor cycle is the core workflow of TDD.

Red

Write a test case.

Run it.

The test fails.

------------------------------------------------------------

Green

Write only the minimum production code required.

Run the test again.

The test passes.

------------------------------------------------------------

Refactor

Improve the code structure without changing its behavior.

All tests should continue to pass.

Memory Trick

R → G → R

Red
↓

Green
↓

Refactor

